{
  "unit": {
    "id": "10",
    "title": "Giving and telling",
    "blurb": "The dative: to whom?",
    "intro": "The dative case marks the person who receives something, the indirect object, “to” or “for”: _pater fīliō librum dat_ (the father gives a book to the son). Endings: first declension _-ae_ / _-īs_, second _-ō_ / _-īs_, third _-ī_ / _-ibus_. The pronouns have their own forms: _mihi_ (to me), _tibi_ (to you), _nōbīs_ (to us), _vōbīs_ (to you all). _Cui?_ asks “to whom?”."
  },
